<p>Your first production decision is also your most consequential: which pr=
oblem deserves a video? A polished explanation of a problem nobody in your =
intended audience currently cares about has a weak starting position. A pla=
in but useful demonstration of a painful problem has something real to impr=
ove. This chapter teaches you to find that second kind of opportunity, turn=
 it into a testable promise, and maintain enough ideas that publishing neve=
r depends on inspiration arriving on Tuesday.</p>
<p>The prerequisite is the audience decision from Chapter 1. The working au=
dience here is developers who know some Python or TypeScript and want to bu=
ild useful AI powered browser or web tools while understanding what makes t=
hem reliable. Your browser extensions, fullstack projects, technical notes,=
 and first principles ML writing make that a credible starting hypothesis. =
Credible does not mean proven demand. This chapter establishes the process =
that tests it.</p>
<p>By the end, you will have three audience problem maps, a ranked backlog,=
 a first twelve video sequence, and a method for choosing next week's video=
 in under an hour. The companion's <a href=3D"file:///C:/Users/amallick.ENG=
INEERS/OneDrive%20-%20Unison%20Software,%20Inc/Documents/ChatGPT/My%20caree=
r%20growth%20and%20side%20hustle%20income/youtube-growth-course/companion/i=
ndex.html#idea-score">idea scorecard</a> lets you change priorities and see=
 how rankings move. Its scores are decision aids, not forecasts of views.</=
p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>1. A topic is not yet an idea</h3>
<p>=E2=80=9CAI tools=E2=80=9D is a topic. =E2=80=9CDevelopers are accepting=
 generated tests that pass without checking the behavior they care about=E2=
=80=9D describes a problem. =E2=80=9CI gave an AI coding assistant a delibe=
rately broken browser extension, then tested whether its fix actually worke=
d=E2=80=9D is an idea. The final sentence contains a viewer, a conflict, an=
 observable method, and a possible outcome. Those ingredients give the view=
er a reason to choose your video over another tab.</p>
<p>Treat an idea as a small contract. The viewer brings time and attention;=
 you promise a useful change in understanding, ability, or judgment. Write =
the contract before you write the script: =E2=80=9CAfter watching, a develo=
per can distinguish valid structured output from correct application behavi=
or and can add one test that catches the difference.=E2=80=9D You can inspe=
ct whether you have fulfilled that contract. You cannot inspect whether you=
 have fulfilled =E2=80=9Ctalk about AI.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>Begin with the moment before the video is needed. A learner has a genera=
ted JSON object, but the application displays the wrong answer. A freelance=
r has a client request for an AI assistant but cannot estimate its running =
cost. A developer has a working extension that loses its state when its bac=
kground process stops. These moments are more useful than broad demographic=
 descriptions because they suggest what to show on screen and what the next=
 useful product might be.</p>
<p>Now separate the problem from your preferred solution. If you love neura=
l networks, you may translate every audience complaint into =E2=80=9Cthey n=
eed an ML fundamentals course.=E2=80=9D Sometimes they need a five line val=
idation function or a clearer mental model of asynchronous work. Start with=
 the smallest satisfying intervention. You can earn permission to teach dee=
per material after the learner sees why it matters.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>2. Build three problem maps before choosing three channels</h3>
<p>The user asked for at least three channel concepts. That does not make l=
aunching three channels at once a good experiment. Three separate feeds spl=
it your learning, publishing capacity, and social proof. Use three concept =
lanes to compare opportunities, then select one audience promise for the fi=
rst twelve long videos. Keep the alternatives available if evidence points =
elsewhere.</p>
