<p>These are original positioning proposals inferred from your public work,=
 rather than a claim that any one will produce a particular revenue. Your <=
a href=3D"https://aadilmallick.com/">portfolio</a> establishes the intersec=
tion of fullstack and AI/ML work; the project descriptions are evidence of =
topics you can investigate, not proof of enterprise results. The <a href=3D=
"https://aadilmallick.com/tools">tools page</a> supplies concrete demonstra=
tion material. Verify the current behavior of a project before filming it.<=
/p>
<p>For each lane, write five rows in a problem map. A row contains the view=
er's words, the triggering event, the current workaround, the cost of remai=
ning stuck, and what satisfying evidence would look like. For example: =E2=
=80=9CMy generated extension keeps forgetting its settings; it happens afte=
r I close and reopen the browser; I reload it until it works; I cannot publ=
ish it confidently; I need a visible reproduction and a fix that survives a=
 restart.=E2=80=9D This row is already closer to a strong video than a page=
 of keywords.</p>
<p>Do not assume the most financially valuable viewer is always the best fi=
rst viewer. An experienced engineering manager may have purchasing power bu=
t expect evidence you have not developed yet. A TypeScript developer buildi=
ng a first extension may be easier to help accurately and may buy useful no=
tes. Start where your demonstrated competence meets a problem you can solve=
 repeatedly. Your authority can expand through the public evidence you crea=
te.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>3. Understand the three kinds of demand</h3>
<p>Search demand starts with an explicit question: =E2=80=9CChrome extensio=
n storage not persisting.=E2=80=9D The viewer already wants a solution. You=
r job is to make the fit obvious and get to the useful answer. Search deman=
d is well suited to early authority building, although some questions have =
a small audience or are answered quickly without producing lasting interest=
 in the channel.</p>
<p>Browse demand starts with a latent concern or curiosity: =E2=80=9CThis e=
xtension worked until I closed the browser.=E2=80=9D A viewer did not type =
the problem but recognizes its significance. The packaging has to make the =
consequence understandable without assuming prior context. This can reach m=
ore people than a narrow error message, but the content must fulfill the wi=
der promise.</p>
<p>Relationship demand starts with trust in you: =E2=80=9CI want to see how=
 Aadil approaches this.=E2=80=9D It supports updates, live sessions, and mo=
re exploratory work. A new channel has relatively little relationship deman=
d, so =E2=80=9Cmy thoughts on the latest model=E2=80=9D asks strangers to c=
are before you have demonstrated why they should. Give them a useful experi=
ment first. Personal perspective becomes more valuable when attached to obs=
erved work.</p>
<p>YouTube describes Search as matching the query and engagement for that q=
uery, while recommendations consider the viewer and the content's relevance=
 and performance. That supports choosing the viewer situation before optimi=
zing metadata. It does not imply that you can mechanically manufacture dist=
ribution with keywords. <a href=3D"https://support.google.com/youtube/answe=
r/141805?hl=3Den">YouTube performance FAQ</a>.</p>
<p>An early portfolio might contain six solution oriented videos, four expe=
riment or comparison videos, and two deeper explanations. Those are curricu=
lum recommendations, not platform requirements. At your starting capacity o=
f eight to ten hours a week, twelve videos can be a roughly six month seaso=
n, not a requirement for the first ninety days. The baseline ninety day pla=
n is six long videos, with additional output only after your measured workf=
low becomes faster. The mix gives you different discovery opportunities whi=
le keeping the same viewer. The extension tutorial, AI code test, and ML ex=
planation belong together only if the same person can reasonably want all t=
hree.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-4"></a></p>
<h3>4. Research without becoming a full time researcher</h3>
<p>Allocate a recurring forty five minute research block. Spend fifteen min=
utes collecting audience problems, fifteen examining existing solutions, an=
d fifteen converting observations into briefs. A time limit prevents resear=
ch from becoming a pleasant substitute for finishing videos. Save unresolve=
d questions instead of opening twenty more tabs.</p>
<p>Start with direct evidence you already encounter: questions on your own =
tutorials, issues in your own repositories, tutoring questions you have per=
mission to generalize, and difficulties from your own building process. Str=
ip private details and use synthetic data. A real problem does not require =
exposing a real customer's environment. Record what failed, why it matters,=
 and whether a small independent demonstration can reproduce it.</p>
<p>Use YouTube search next. Type the problem in ordinary language, then one=
 technical variation. Examine results for fit, age, scope, and the skill le=
vel assumed. The presence of existing videos is not a reason to stop; it es=
tablishes that someone has invested in addressing the problem. Your opening=
 may be narrower, more current, more testable, easier to follow, or connect=
ed to a concrete tool.</p>
<p>Inspect comments as qualitative research, not a representative survey. A=
 comment saying =E2=80=9Cplease explain deployment=E2=80=9D tells you one v=
isible viewer asked. It does not tell you how many silent viewers need it. =
Save an anonymized paraphrase and the public source link for your own resea=
rch. Look for repeated obstacles across independent places, and avoid treat=
ing emotionally vivid complaints as necessarily common.</p>
<p>The current official Studio route is <strong>Insights =E2=86=92 Research=
</strong> on desktop. Its documented filters include content type, upload d=
ate, subscriber count, duration, channel age, outlier score, and what your =
viewers watched elsewhere. Older accounts or rollout states may still expos=
e a Trends surface; YouTube's older help page explicitly flags changes begi=
nning July 2026. Use whichever your account actually shows. <a href=3D"http=
s://support.google.com/youtube/answer/17232321">Research content and find t=
rends</a>, <a href=3D"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/11962757?hl=
=3Den">Trends rollout note</a>.</p>
<p>If a new channel has limited audience data, do not interpret an empty au=
dience report as zero demand. Use the platform's broader results, direct qu=
estions, and your initial publishing tests. Native research is an observati=
on tool; it cannot tell you whether you can deliver a convincing demonstrat=
ion or whether its viewers will buy your notes.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-5"></a></p>
<h3>5. Read competitor evidence carefully</h3>
<p>Record the title, publication date, duration, broad format, visible prem=
ise, and public view count at the time you inspect a result. Keep your obse=
rvation date. Public numbers change, and videos of different ages have had =
different opportunities to accumulate views. Avoid comparing a two year eve=
rgreen tutorial with yesterday's news as if they ran the same experiment.</=
p>
<p>For a manual relative performance proxy, choose a video's public views a=
nd divide by the median views of a reasonable set of recent, comparable vid=
eos on that same channel. If the selected video has 24,000 views and the co=
mparable median is 8,000, the proxy is three. Median means the middle value=
 after ordering the observations; it is less distorted by one giant hit tha=
n an average. This is your approximation, not a reconstruction of YouTube's=
 native outlier formula.</p>
<p>Exclude incomparable livestream archives, Shorts, and unusually old uplo=
ads when making that small comparison. Write down every exclusion so you do=
 not secretly choose a comparison set that supports your favorite idea. A t=
hree times result may reflect a topic, an unusually strong title, a collabo=
ration, outside promotion, or something you cannot see. It identifies a can=
didate worth studying; it does not identify a cause.</p>
<p>Look at misses as well as hits. If a creator has five AI tool comparison=
s and only one attracted broad attention, ask what differentiates its viewe=
r problem. Do not copy the single title and conclude that all comparisons w=
ork. A useful research set includes at least a few ordinary performers from=
 the same creator. Learning only from winners produces a distorted sense of=
 how reliably a format succeeds.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-6"></a></p>
<h3>6. Use a scorecard that rewards evidence</h3>
<p>Rate each candidate from one to five on six dimensions: audience pain, o=
bserved demand, proof you can show, fit with the channel promise, useful ne=
xt step, and production feasibility. A five in proof means you can already =
reproduce the relevant behavior. A one means the idea relies on a claim you=
 have not checked. Feasibility is high when you can finish within the weekl=
y capacity, not when the idea sounds impressive.</p>
<p>Use this starting formula: weighted score equals three times pain plus t=
wo times demand plus three times proof plus two times fit plus two times us=
eful next step plus feasibility. The highest possible result is sixty five.=
 These weights are an editorial preference for useful, demonstrable work. T=
hey have no statistical relationship to expected views. Change them when yo=
ur actual constraint changes.</p>
<p>Consider three candidates. =E2=80=9CThe ten best AI tools=E2=80=9D score=
s pain two, demand four, proof two, fit three, next step two, feasibility t=
hree. Its result is thirty three. =E2=80=9CI tested whether generated exten=
sion code survives a restart=E2=80=9D scores four, three, five, five, four,=
 four. Its result is fifty five. =E2=80=9CBuild a transformer from scratch =
over eight hours=E2=80=9D scores four, three, two, three, four, one, yieldi=
ng thirty nine. The second idea wins because you can show a specific, valua=
ble result within your capacity.</p>
<p>If your enthusiasm disagrees with the score, inspect the assumptions. Yo=
u may have underrated personal motivation, or overestimated demand from a f=
ew popular videos. Add a short note rather than gaming every score until yo=
ur favorite wins. A scorecard should expose reasoning to future review. It =
should not let a spreadsheet pretend to know what only publishing can revea=
l.</p>
<p>Maintain a separate evidence grade: <strong>A</strong> for repeated dire=
ct questions plus a working reproduction; <strong>B</strong> for several pl=
ausible public signals and a feasible demonstration; <strong>C</strong> for=
 your untested hypothesis. A high score with grade C is an experiment, not =
your safest next upload. Keep a mix: mostly well supported ideas, with room=
 for a small number of ambitious tests.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-7"></a></p>
<h3>7. Turn one problem into a content cluster</h3>
<p>Suppose you choose =E2=80=9CAI generated extension code works once but f=
ails later.=E2=80=9D Make the primary video a clear experiment: build a tin=
y extension, run a lifecycle test, inspect the failure, and show the correc=
ted design. Its main promise is reliability. The free supporting resource i=
s the test checklist. The technical notes product can contain the broader a=
rchitecture patterns, if you actually develop and maintain them.</p>
<p>The same research can support a short explanation of why background work=
 can stop, a follow up on persistent storage, and a deeper tutorial on sepa=
rating interface state from durable data. These are not four copies of the =
same video. Each answers a different question at a useful level. Avoid stre=
tching one small lesson into a month merely to fill the calendar.</p>
<p>Create the sequence in dependency order. Show the failure before you tea=
ch the abstraction. Explain what must survive before comparing storage mech=
anisms. Teach how to verify persistence before discussing automated testing=
. This order gives viewers reasons to continue into the next video because =
each one resolves a problem created or clarified by the previous one.</p>
<p>Do not turn every cluster into a serialized course that requires episode=
 one. A viewer should understand the current video's problem without homewo=
rk. Use a twenty second recap and link the prerequisite for those who want =
more. Your library can be coherent without making discovery feel like joini=
ng a class halfway through a semester.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-8"></a></p>
<h3>8. Seventy two original video ideas</h3>
<p>These are proposed working premises, not reports of tests already perfor=
med. Difficulty refers to production and demonstration complexity: <strong>=
L</strong> means a focused demonstration; <strong>M</strong> requires a tes=
ted small project; <strong>H</strong> requires substantial evaluation, visu=
al teaching, or multiple environments. Business intent describes the releva=
nt next step, not permission to sell before the resource exists. The three =
lanes should be tested on paper before choosing your initial channel promis=
e.</p>
