<p>These premises deliberately avoid promising salaries, universal time sav=
ings, or =E2=80=9Cbest model=E2=80=9D rankings you cannot support. They mak=
e technical work visible. Replace an outcome driven title with a neutral ex=
periment title if the result has not happened. You earn a claim by running =
the test, not by needing a more clickable sentence.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>9. Select a first twelve video season</h3>
<p>For the recommended practical AI engineering lane, choose ideas 1, 2, 8,=
 14, 25, 26, 3, 13, 23, 17, 40, and 72 as a candidate season. This mixes AI=
 evaluation with your stronger concrete browser project evidence. It also g=
ives the learner an arc: define correctness, reproduce failures, build a re=
liable tool, then judge more complex systems. Review feasibility before com=
mitting to the last two.</p>
<p>The first video should make the channel's promise unmistakable. =E2=80=
=9CThe AI returned valid JSON. My app was still wrong=E2=80=9D can show a s=
ynthetic note extraction output that satisfies its shape but assigns a quot=
e to the wrong source. The viewer sees the bug, learns the distinction betw=
een format and meaning, and leaves with one test. You do not need a broad i=
ntroduction to the channel first.</p>
<p>The second should demonstrate that your first video's rigor is a repeata=
ble habit. A small set of extension tests gives you an observable protocol,=
 while the third can explore a missed bug. When the sixth video discusses a=
 browser lifecycle issue, connect it explicitly to the same audience concer=
n: generated code needs systems understanding. That connection prevents the=
 channel from feeling like an unrelated collection of interests.</p>
<p>For a browser engineering season, start with ideas 25, 33, 34, 26, 32, 3=
8, 41, 44, 27, 28, 40, and 48. For an ML teaching season, start with 49, 50=
, 55, 64, 59, 53, 54, 51, 60, 57, 58, and 69. Choose one as the main promis=
e rather than interleaving three entirely different beginner sequences.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>10. Write a one page brief before committing</h3>
<p>A brief is the bridge between research and scripting. Give it a working =
title, intended viewer, problem, promised result, required proof, opening d=
emonstration, three teaching beats, useful next action, production limit, a=
nd stop condition. A stop condition states what would make the premise inva=
lid. For an AI benchmark, it might be =E2=80=9CI cannot create a fair task =
set within two hours,=E2=80=9D which signals that the current video is too =
broad.</p>
<p>Here is a complete brief in prose. The viewer is a TypeScript developer =
building a note extraction feature. The problem is that valid structured da=
ta can still contain a source error. The promise is to add a small evaluati=
on that catches one meaningful failure. The proof is a synthetic dataset wi=
th expected sources, the generated output, and a failing assertion before t=
he fix. The opening shows the wrong source highlighted beside valid JSON. T=
he body explains shape, behavior, and test design. The ending offers the de=
monstrated checklist and a follow up on regression tests. Total production =
allowance is ten hours. No reliability percentage is promised from a tiny d=
ataset.</p>
<p>This brief does two things a topic list cannot. It defines the evidence =
you need before recording, and it makes the editing target visible. If a se=
ction does not help the viewer understand or reproduce the evaluation, it p=
robably belongs in a different video or in the downloadable notes. You can =
still include personality, but it should help the viewer remain oriented.</=
p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-14"></a></p>
<h3>11. Develop ideas with AI without outsourcing judgment</h3>
<p>An assistant is useful for generating alternative audience questions, fi=
nding missing assumptions, and turning a vague premise into several testabl=
e versions. Give it your actual audience, available evidence, and capacity.=
 A prompt that says =E2=80=9Cgive me viral AI video ideas=E2=80=9D supplies=
 almost none of the constraints that make a useful idea yours.</p>
<p>Use a prompt such as: =E2=80=9CI teach TypeScript developers to ship rel=
iable AI browser tools. I can demonstrate a timestamp bookmarker, a screen =
recorder, and a synthetic note extractor. Generate twelve specific viewer p=
roblems. For each, give a claim I can test in one afternoon, the evidence n=
eeded, one misconception, and a relevant free worksheet. Do not invent resu=
lts, audience statistics, income, or current product capabilities. Flag whe=
re primary documentation is required.=E2=80=9D This is a prompt for your la=
ter work, not a research action already performed.</p>
<p>Then select three suggestions and challenge them. Ask what a skeptical d=
eveloper would say, what would make the result misleading, and whether the =
same question can be answered more clearly without AI. Check documentation =
directly and run the technical demonstration yourself. A language model can=
 generate plausible premises that collapse under testing. Catching that bef=
ore filming is an advantage of your engineering background.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-15"></a></p>
<h3>12. Keep the backlog healthy</h3>
<p>Maintain five states: inbox, researched, demonstrable, scripted, and pub=
lished. Do not fill the scripted column with thirty videos whose topics wil=
l become stale. Keep a larger inbox and a small ready queue. Two demonstrab=
le evergreen ideas and one timely candidate are a useful starting buffer fo=
r a solo creator with a job.</p>
<p>Once a month, retire ideas that no longer fit. An idea can be interestin=
g and still attract a different audience from the one you are building. Sav=
e it in an archive with a reason rather than repeatedly reconsidering it. T=
his reduces the emotional cost of saying no. Your backlog exists to reduce =
decisions, not create a permanent collection of obligations.</p>
<p>After each release, add actual audience language to the next brief. A co=
mment asking =E2=80=9CHow would this work with multiple tabs?=E2=80=9D is a=
 concrete possible extension. If several viewers misunderstand the same ter=
m, clarify it in the next video rather than assuming they need a separate h=
our long course. Small, direct follow ups can teach you more than a large s=
peculative series.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-02-section-16"></a></p>
<h3>13. Application project and acceptance checks</h3>
<p>Set aside two sessions. In the first, write fifteen problem rows across =
the three lanes and collect ten public observations with dates and direct l=
inks. In the second, score twelve ideas, reproduce the evidence for your to=
p two, and write a complete brief for one. You are producing a usable edito=
rial decision, not trying to prove that your first niche choice is permanen=
t.</p>
<p>A finished backlog has a named viewer for every ready idea; a visible re=
sult the viewer will receive; a feasible demonstration; a production limit;=
 an appropriate next step; and a note distinguishing observed demand from y=
our hypothesis. A brief fails review if its title requires a result you hav=
e not obtained, if the viewer changes halfway through, or if its product pi=
tch solves a different problem from the lesson.</p>
<p>Use the companion's <a href=3D"file:///C:/Users/amallick.ENGINEERS/OneDr=
ive%20-%20Unison%20Software,%20Inc/Documents/ChatGPT/My%20career%20growth%2=
0and%20side%20hustle%20income/youtube-growth-course/companion/index.html#id=
ea-score">idea scorecard</a> to compare a safe evergreen tutorial with an a=
mbitious experiment. Change feasibility and proof scores, then explain why =
the ranking changed. The meaningful learning is the tradeoff you can articu=
late. No calculated score can substitute for the next published video and t=
he response it receives.</p>
<p>The handoff to Chapter 3 is a single sentence: =E2=80=9CThis specific vi=
ewer should choose this specific promise because I can show this specific p=
roof.=E2=80=9D With that sentence, titles and thumbnails become a communica=
tion problem you can solve. Without it, packaging work tends to become deco=
ration around a vague topic.</p>
