<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>Editing is the design of the final explanation</h3>
<p>You have recorded a good project demonstration, a few narration mistakes=
, a camera introduction, and several useful test cases. They are raw materi=
al. Editing decides what the viewer encounters, in what order, and for how =
long. Its first job is to make the promised lesson understandable. Color ef=
fects, animated captions, and AI-generated footage come after that job, and=
 some videos will not need them at all.</p>
<p>For your first six long videos, use a deliberately small visual vocabula=
ry: readable screen evidence, clean narration, occasional face-to-camera ju=
dgment, a few labels, and simple diagrams. That is enough to explain an eng=
ineering project well. Your eight-to-ten-hour weekly capacity makes it espe=
cially important to learn the small set of editing actions that produce mos=
t of the result before exploring every page of a professional editor.</p>
<p>This chapter uses DaVinci Resolve as the free-first learning path on Win=
dows. The official site identified Resolve 21 during research on November 1=
3, 2026, while several official training books remained labeled Resolve 20.=
 That difference is a version note, not an instruction to install an older =
release. Use the training for concepts and verify any changed control in yo=
ur installed version. The course does not claim that Resolve or the other t=
ools below were installed or operated on your device. <a href=3D"https://ww=
w.blackmagicdesign.com/products/davinciresolve/training">Blackmagic Design =
training</a></p>
<p>The end product is a six-to-ten-minute video that tells the truth, sound=
s consistent, displays readable evidence, contains corrected captions, and =
fits a repeatable production routine. The optional Short is a derivative of=
 a complete insight, not an obligation to run a second production line. We =
will also build a method for evaluating modern AI tools so that a growing s=
ubscription stack does not become a substitute for editing skill.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>Choose an editor by the work you need to finish</h3>
<p>An editor is a workspace for arranging media over time. A timeline repre=
sents that time from left to right. Video tracks hold pictures, overlays, a=
nd labels; audio tracks hold narration, application sound, music, and effec=
ts. The viewer sees the composite picture and hears the mix. Learning this =
model makes the interface less mysterious: you are placing evidence and spe=
ech along a shared clock.</p>
<p>DaVinci Resolve provides a capable general editing path and room to lear=
n audio and color. Its complexity can slow a first session, so keep the ini=
tial task narrow. Learn import, timeline creation, selection, split, trim, =
ripple delete, zoom, text, audio level adjustment, and export. A ripple ope=
ration closes or opens timeline space as an edit changes duration. You do n=
ot need advanced compositing to remove a retake or highlight a field.</p>
<p>If Resolve performs poorly on your actual hardware or feels like too muc=
h interface for the first sample, use a simpler editor you can reliably exp=
ort from. Microsoft's <a href=3D"https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/clipch=
amp/">Clipchamp help and learning</a> is a current official starting point =
for a simpler Windows-oriented option. The tradeoff is not moral or profess=
ional status: a simpler tool may finish straightforward videos faster, whil=
e a larger editor offers more control as the work becomes complex. Test one=
 representative sample instead of comparing feature lists indefinitely.</p>
<p>If you already pay for Adobe Premiere and can use it comfortably, stayin=
g with it may save time. Adobe documents <a href=3D"https://www.adobe.com/p=
roducts/premiere/editing-and-trimming.html">text-based editing and trimming=
</a>. You do not need to switch because another creator uses a different ed=
itor. Use one main editor for the first six videos and evaluate a change on=
ly when you can name a concrete limitation and measure the cost of migratio=
n.</p>
<p>Do not choose by the number of AI features. Choose by whether the tool c=
an preserve your readable code, handle your audio tracks, support accurate =
captions, and produce the final file without recurring problems. A thirty-s=
econd test project containing real screen capture, your microphone track, a=
nd one camera shot is a much better comparison than a promotional demo.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>Prepare the project before cutting</h3>
<p>Use the folder structure from the filming chapter. Keep original recordi=
ngs unchanged in source folders. The editor project is usually a set of ins=
tructions referencing those files, not a replacement for them. Moving or re=
naming media after import can break links. If you need to reorganize, do it=
 deliberately and learn the editor's relinking process rather than treating=
 a missing-media warning as destroyed footage.</p>
<p>Create a project with a meaningful video identifier and set the intended=
 timeline resolution and frame rate before substantial editing. For the cou=
rse baseline, that is a horizontal 1920-by-1080, 30-fps SDR timeline if you=
r source and tested workflow match it. A timeline is the working sequence; =
an export is the delivered file created from it. Keeping those concepts sep=
arate prevents the common assumption that changing an export setting automa=
tically repairs a mismatched source or timeline.</p>
<p>Import the screen, camera, and audio files into distinct media bins. A b=
in is an organizational collection within the project. Name or label the us=
eful takes based on the shot list: opening result, missing-answer failure, =
accepted structure, false-card pass, review interface, and closing. You can=
 organize without watching every second of every clip, but inspect the crit=
ical result shots before investing in a long edit.</p>
<p>Duplicate the initial timeline or save a version before major structural=
 changes. Use meaningful version names such as =E2=80=9Crough explanation,=
=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cevidence placed,=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cfinal mix.=E2=80=
=9D Autosave is useful, but a deliberate milestone makes it easier to retur=
n to an earlier narrative when a later experiment fails. Export a project b=
ackup after the editing session according to the editor's supported workflo=
w and keep it with the video folder.</p>
<p>Write the video's promise at the top of your edit note. For the recurrin=
g example: =E2=80=9CA developer understands what structural validation catc=
hes and why it does not prove a study card is true.=E2=80=9D When deciding =
whether to keep a beautiful but irrelevant sequence, return to that sentenc=
e. The timeline should make the promise easier to fulfill, not accumulate e=
very asset you recorded.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-4"></a></p>
<h3>A first edit you can complete in one sitting</h3>
<p>Use the ninety-second sample rather than your entire first project for t=
his guided pass. Allow thirty to sixty minutes while learning. The point is=
 to perform the complete sequence once, not to master the interface. Keep t=
he <a href=3D"https://documents.blackmagicdesign.com/UserManuals/DaVinci_Re=
solve_21.1_Reference_Manual.pdf">official Resolve manual</a> available for =
your installed version. The current manual's setup sections were checked fo=
r project settings and keyboard customization; this course does not claim a=
 full manual audit. If you use a custom keyboard preset, identify commands =
by their names and tooltips rather than assuming a tutorial's shortcuts mat=
ch yours.</p>
<ol>
<li>Create a new project with a unique practice name. Open the bottom-right=
 project-settings gear and review Master Settings. Set the intended resolut=
ion and frame rate before building the sample sequence, then save.</li>
<li>Go to the Edit workspace. Import the sample media into the Media Pool a=
nd drag the main recording onto a new timeline. Play it once and confirm th=
e intended audio is present.</li>
<li>Locate the start and end of a retake. Use the blade/split tool to separ=
ate that interval, return to selection, and select the unwanted segment. Ap=
ply Ripple Delete so later material closes the gap. Play across the join.</=
li>
<li>Place your result shot on a video track above the narration. Trim its e=
dges so it appears during the sentence describing the result. Keep the usef=
ul narration underneath and mute unwanted sound from the overlay.</li>
<li>Add a basic text label, set its wording and size in the available text =
controls, and place it where it does not cover the proof. Adjust the narrat=
ion level, then save.</li>
<li>Open Deliver, choose your tested export settings, set a new output file=
name, add the job to the render queue, and start the render. Open the expor=
ted file outside the editor.</li>
</ol>
<p>These actions expose the core relationships. Splitting creates separate =
editable segments without deleting the original recording. Ripple deletion =
changes timing, which is why you immediately review the join. An upper vide=
o track can cover the main picture while leaving narration audible; the tra=
ck's position and its sound are separate choices. A render job describes th=
e export, while starting the queue actually creates it. If a step fails, yo=
u now know which relationship to inspect instead of treating the entire app=
lication as mysterious.</p>
<p>Make two intentional mistakes in the practice copy so you can recognize =
them later. Move the result shot five seconds early and listen to how confu=
sing the explanation becomes. Restore it, then make the label large enough =
to cover the answer field and see why readable evidence matters more than d=
ecoration. Undo each change before the final export. These are optional sma=
ll experiments, not a requirement to demonstrate mastery before continuing.=
</p>
<p>When the sample works, write down the commands that felt slow and learn =
only those shortcuts. Open Keyboard Customization and search the command na=
me if you need to see or change its mapping. Learning three actions you use=
 fifty times is more useful than memorizing a page of rarely used key combi=
nations. Keep the first successful sample as a reference and use a fresh pr=
oject for the real video.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-5"></a></p>
<h3>Pass one: assemble the argument</h3>
<p>Build a rough sequence before adding polish. Put the best narration and =
essential evidence in the order of the script. Remove obvious retakes, dead=
 time, unrelated tangents, and repeated explanations. Keep enough pauses fo=
r the new idea to land. At this stage, a basic cut is usually sufficient. A=
 cut moves directly from one shot to the next; a dissolve blends them over =
time. Use a dissolve when it communicates a transition, not to conceal ever=
y edit.</p>
<p>In the extension video, the rough sequence should show the false card, i=
ntroduce the small system, demonstrate a missing field, insert the structur=
al boundary, run the two test cases, explain the remaining semantic problem=
, and give the next action. If that sequence is confusing without music, th=
e problem is probably the explanation or missing evidence. Decorative polis=
h is unlikely to solve it.</p>
<p>Listen to the rough cut without watching. Does each sentence follow logi=
cally? Does =E2=80=9Cthis=E2=80=9D have a clear referent? Are you introduci=
ng a term before explaining it? If a sentence says the app =E2=80=9Cverifie=
s the response,=E2=80=9D decide whether it means structure or meaning. Edit=
ing is another opportunity to remove an overclaim that slipped through the =
script. Record a replacement line rather than trying to repair the distinct=
ion with a tiny disclaimer viewers may miss.</p>
<p>Then watch without sound. Can you identify the result and follow the seq=
uence of visual changes? A viewer cannot fully learn the lesson without nar=
ration, but the pictures should relate to the ideas rather than function as=
 unrelated decoration. If the same tiny code file remains on screen during =
three different concepts, plan a close view, diagram, or comparison that ca=
rries each concept more clearly.</p>
<p>Do not judge a rough cut against a highly polished creator's final video=
. Judge whether it contains a coherent explanation. The first pass is compl=
ete when every necessary beat exists in the right order and the biggest mis=
sing material is identified. Make a short pickup list: one replacement sent=
ence, one missing output shot, one static diagram. Collect those specific i=
tems before getting lost in fonts and animation.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-6"></a></p>
<h3>Pass two: align narration with evidence</h3>
<p>Put the relevant evidence on screen when the narration names it. If you =
say =E2=80=9Cthe answer field is missing,=E2=80=9D the missing field compar=
ison should already be visible or appear at that moment. Do not require vie=
wers to remember an earlier shot while reading unrelated code. This alignme=
nt is especially important for engineering audiences because the visual oft=
en contains the actual proof.</p>
<p>Use B-roll deliberately. B-roll is supplementary imagery placed over the=
 main audio or primary scene. On your channel, useful B-roll is often your =
own application, a close view of a test result, a diagram, or a short relev=
ant screen action. It does not have to be cinematic footage of a laptop. Ge=
neric AI-generated computers may make the video look busy while providing n=
o evidence about the program you are explaining.</p>
<p>When moving between your face and a screen, let the speech continue natu=
rally if the visual change supports the same thought. An L-cut keeps the pr=
evious shot's audio under the next picture; a J-cut introduces the next aud=
io before its picture. You do not need to memorize the names to use the ide=
a. The reason to overlap is continuity: the viewer follows the sentence whi=
le their attention moves to the relevant evidence.</p>
<p>Hold a readable result long enough to inspect. The exact time depends on=
 complexity, not a universal rule. For a one-line error, a few seconds may =
be enough. For two cards compared with a source excerpt, the viewer needs l=
onger. Read the material yourself at a normal pace and allow margin. If the=
 hold feels slow, simplify the amount of text rather than racing past an es=
sential proof shot.</p>
<p>Use freeze frames or a still capture when motion is irrelevant and extra=
 reading time helps. Label the state accurately if needed. If you are showi=
ng a mockup of a planned review interface, say =E2=80=9Cprototype design=E2=
=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cmockup=E2=80=9D visibly and in narration. Editing should=
 preserve the distinction between something that ran, something you intenti=
onally staged, and something you propose to build.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-7"></a></p>
<h3>Make code readable without turning it into a light show</h3>
<p>Crop to the code that matters, but preserve enough context to avoid misl=
eading the viewer. A ten-line excerpt can focus attention; omitting the con=
dition that changes its meaning can distort the explanation. Keep the full =
runnable example or relevant source available with the project when the vid=
eo intentionally skips setup. If no code is being distributed, state that t=
he screen is an explanatory excerpt.</p>
<p>Use restrained highlighting. A box around the answer field, a subtle dim=
ming of unrelated lines, or a single arrow can guide attention. Add the hig=
hlight when the narration reaches that concept, then remove it when its job=
 is done. Five simultaneous arrows force the viewer to decide which one mat=
ters. Consistent colors can help distinguish input, check, and output, but =
do not rely on color alone; label the roles too.</p>
<p>Animate zooms only when they help orientation. Begin with enough context=
 to show where the relevant code lives, move toward the detail, then hold s=
till while explaining it. Constant motion makes reading difficult. If the s=
ource is only 1080p and you enlarge it heavily, the text may soften; prepar=
e a higher-resolution source or a clean explanatory card rather than using =
sharpen filters to recover information that was never captured.</p>
<p>For a mathematical explanation, build the equation in understandable sta=
ges. Show the example's numbers, identify what each represents, then show t=
he compact formula. An animation that writes every symbol without explainin=
g the relationship can look educational while leaving the viewer lost. The =
same editing principle applies to architecture diagrams: reveal the compone=
nt when it becomes necessary, and keep the surrounding model stable enough =
to follow.</p>
<p>Create two or three reusable visual elements after the first finished vi=
deo: a source-versus-output comparison, a code highlight, and a result labe=
l. Reuse them to establish familiarity and reduce editing time. Do not crea=
te a large motion graphics package before you know which elements your less=
ons actually need. The reusable system should emerge from observed work, no=
t become a separate project that delays publishing.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-8"></a></p>
<h3>Pass three: shape the sound</h3>
<p>Speech is the primary information channel for most of your videos. Start=
 by making the narration consistent. Remove unusable takes and distracting =
accidental noise, reduce abrupt level changes between sections, and add sma=
ll fades where edits create clicks. Listen at a comfortable volume through =
headphones, then through ordinary speakers. A mix that works only through e=
xpensive headphones may fail your audience's everyday viewing conditions.</=
p>
<p>Use the raw track as the reference. If you apply noise reduction or AI e=
nhancement, compare processed and original versions at similar loudness. Lo=
uder often feels better even when it is more distorted, so a fair compariso=
n matters. Listen particularly to consonants, quiet word endings, breaths, =
and technical terms. Excessive processing can make speech sound watery or r=
obotic. A small amount of room sound is often preferable to damaged words.<=
/p>
<p>Compression reduces differences between louder and quieter material; it =
does not make unclear speech meaningful. Equalization changes the balance o=
f frequency ranges; it does not remove every room reflection. A limiter con=
trols excessive output peaks; it does not repair clipped source audio. Lear=
n one tool at a time against a named problem. Resolve's <a href=3D"https://=
www.blackmagicdesign.com/products/davinciresolve/fairlight">Fairlight overv=
iew</a> is an official route into its audio workspace, and the training pag=
e provides dedicated audio lessons.</p>
<p>For a starting delivery practice, aim for speech that feels consistent w=
ith similar videos at a normal player setting and leaves peak headroom. You=
 may use an integrated loudness target around =E2=88=9214 to =E2=88=9216 LU=
FS and a true-peak ceiling around =E2=88=921 dBTP as a practical experiment=
, not a YouTube upload requirement. LUFS estimates perceived loudness over =
time; true peak estimates peaks that can appear during reconstruction. The =
exact target is less important than clear, undistorted, consistent speech. =
Check with a meter and your ears rather than assuming normalization makes e=
very mix good.</p>
<p>Add music only if it improves the experience. Keep it low enough that th=
e words remain effortless to understand, reduce it further under dense expl=
anations, and consider no music during important comparisons. A tutorial ca=
n be engaging without a continuous soundtrack. If you use sound effects, ma=
ke them purposeful and gentle. A surprising loud effect can be more disrupt=
ive than the edit it is meant to emphasize.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-9"></a></p>
<h3>Pass four: make the picture consistent</h3>
<p>Color correction aims for a coherent, believable picture; grading applie=
s a deliberate style. Your initial camera clips need correction more than a=
 dramatic style. Match exposure and white balance across adjacent shots so =
your face does not jump from orange to blue. Keep skin and screen content n=
atural enough that the viewer focuses on the explanation. Resolve's <a href=
=3D"https://www.blackmagicdesign.com/products/davinciresolve/color">color o=
verview</a> provides the official entry point; advanced grading is optional=
 for this course.</p>
<p>Do not apply a cinematic lookup table to a code screen merely because it=
 looks attractive on camera footage. A lookup table maps colors to other co=
lors and may reduce the clarity of interface text or change the meaning of =
highlighted states. Your test result's green and red labels should remain l=
egible, and text should retain sufficient contrast. A screen tutorial has d=
ifferent visual needs from a travel montage.</p>
<p>If a phone clip looks washed out or unusually contrasty, inspect its rec=
ording color format and the editor's interpretation before adjusting slider=
s at random. HDR source in an SDR workflow can require appropriate color ma=
nagement. The filming chapter's standard SDR baseline reduces this complexi=
ty for early videos. When you deliberately choose HDR later, learn the comp=
lete capture-to-delivery path and test it rather than mixing formats casual=
ly.</p>
<p>Keep framing consistent across face segments. A small crop can align two=
 takes, but avoid extreme enlargement that makes one look visibly softer. C=
over an unavoidable jump cut with relevant screen evidence when it serves t=
he sentence. Do not add unrelated stock footage just to hide the fact that =
you edited. Viewers generally understand that an explanation is assembled; =
they need continuity and clarity, not the illusion of a single flawless tak=
e.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-10"></a></p>
<h3>Captions, labels, and accessible explanations</h3>
<p>Captions transcribe speech and relevant sound information so that the co=
ntent is accessible without relying entirely on hearing. On-screen labels i=
dentify a concept or object. They have different jobs. A label reading =E2=
=80=9Cstructure accepted=E2=80=9D can remain concise while the caption cont=
ains the full spoken explanation. Filling the frame with both a dense subti=
tle paragraph and a dense code example can overwhelm the viewer.</p>
<p>Generate a transcript if your tool supports it, then correct it manually=
. Technical terms, names, identifiers, and similar-sounding words need spec=
ial attention. A caption that changes =E2=80=9Ccannot=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9C=
can=E2=80=9D reverses meaning. Search the transcript for important phrases =
such as schema, timestamp, JSON, and your chosen tool names. Correct punctu=
ation and timing so clauses remain understandable. Automatic output is a dr=
aft, not completed accessibility work.</p>
<p>When possible, provide a separate caption track for long videos so viewe=
rs can control it. Burned-in captions can be useful for a Short or an inten=
tional visual style, but they are less flexible. Keep text inside safe area=
s away from interface overlays and test on a phone. Use a readable typeface=
, adequate contrast, and short units of text. Do not use color alone to ind=
icate success or failure.</p>
<p>Describe the meaningful visual evidence in speech. =E2=80=9CThis respons=
e is rejected because the answer is missing=E2=80=9D communicates more than=
 =E2=80=9Clook at this.=E2=80=9D If a diagram's arrows carry the argument, =
explain the relationship verbally. This improves accessibility and helps vi=
ewers who momentarily look away. It also reveals whether your own understan=
ding depends on an unexplained visual shortcut.</p>
