rsal law. If your existing notes already receive specific purchase requests=
, test that product first.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-08-section-4"></a></p>
<h3>Build a lead magnet that earns its place</h3>
<p>A lead magnet is a free resource offered in exchange for an explicit ema=
il relationship. The exchange must be understandable. =E2=80=9CGet my free =
checklist and a weekly practical engineering email=E2=80=9D is clearer than=
 a button that hides the ongoing subscription. You can provide a freely acc=
essible version without an email gate as well; collecting addresses is not =
always worth the friction.</p>
<p>For this channel, draft a two-page <em>Small Browser Tool Launch Checkli=
st</em>. Page one covers the smallest demonstrable outcome: input, state, o=
utput, and recovery. Page two covers the path to a usable release: permissi=
ons, reproducible setup, empty states, failure messages, keyboard access, a=
nd a record of known limitations. For the AI bookmark-to-study pilot, add a=
 small output-review box: expected structure, traceable source claims, and =
behavior when evidence is missing. That aligns the extension artifact with =
the AI-output checklist proposed elsewhere in this course; choose one versi=
on that matches the actual episode. Do not call it a security certification=
 or a guarantee of store approval. It is a practical checklist informed by =
your own projects.</p>
<p>Each item needs an action and a visible result. =E2=80=9CTest persistenc=
e=E2=80=9D is too vague. =E2=80=9CCreate a note, reload the page, reopen th=
e tool, and confirm that the saved text and timestamp remain=E2=80=9D is us=
able. =E2=80=9CHandle errors=E2=80=9D becomes =E2=80=9CDisable the network =
or use an invalid input; the interface explains what failed and offers a re=
covery path.=E2=80=9D The viewer can now perform the check without needing =
your interpretation.</p>
<p>Include a miniature completed example. A synthetic bookmark record might=
 contain a video identifier, a timestamp, and a note. The checklist shows a=
 successful save, then a reload, then the same record restored. It also sho=
ws a deliberately invalid timestamp and the message the user receives. This=
 makes the free resource an extension of the lesson rather than a decorativ=
e PDF.</p>
<p>Keep scope small enough to finish in one focused session. A 120-page =E2=
=80=9Cultimate AI guide=E2=80=9D creates an enormous update obligation and =
makes it hard to understand what attracted a subscriber. A two-page checkli=
st can answer a precise question: does the audience want help shipping smal=
l tools responsibly? If many people request it but ask only about general A=
I news, your positioning or call to action may be attracting the wrong inte=
nt.</p>
<p>Give the resource a version and an update date. Put code-specific assump=
tions in the resource: browser tested, example repository commit if applica=
ble, and known missing features. The version is a service to the reader, no=
t a suggestion that you must update every week. Retire or revise resources =
when their core instructions stop working. A free resource can still damage=
 trust if it repeatedly sends people into broken setup steps.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-08-section-5"></a></p>
<h3>Write a landing page with one job</h3>
<p>A landing page should help a relevant viewer decide whether to request t=
he resource. It needs less navigation than your portfolio because the visit=
or already has a specific context. Use the same language as the video. If t=
he video is about a timestamp extension, the page should immediately mentio=
n that kind of project, not present a broad consultancy homepage.</p>
<p>Here is a complete draft of the central page copy. The resource and clai=
ms must be true before you publish it:</p>
