<p><a id=3D"chapter-09-section-9"></a></p>
<h3>Demand arithmetic: where do sixty notebook orders come from?</h3>
<p>Start with a conversion chain that has defined denominators and a time w=
indow. Suppose a mature channel has 200,000 relevant monthly video views. S=
uppose 2% of those view events produce unique landing-page visits after ded=
uplication within your measurement convention. That gives 4,000 visits. Sup=
pose 30% confirm subscription, producing 1,200 new confirmed subscribers. S=
uppose 5% of that cohort buys the notebook within the chosen observation wi=
ndow. That produces sixty orders if each buyer places one order.</p>
<p>The multiplication is 200,000 =C3=97 0.02 =C3=97 0.30 =C3=97 0.05 =3D 60=
. Gross notes revenue is then 60 =C3=97 $49 =3D $2,940. Each percentage is =
a hypothetical assumption. The chain is useful because it shows the depende=
ncies; it does not establish that your audience will behave this way.</p>
<p>There are important simplifications. Views are events, while landing vis=
itors are people or browser identifiers under a particular analytics defini=
tion. Someone can watch several videos before visiting. Some buyers come fr=
om the existing email list, not the new subscriber cohort. Some subscribe i=
n one month and buy later. Therefore do not treat the chain as a literal ac=
counting reconciliation unless you actually track a consistent cohort and a=
ttribution rule.</p>
<p>For planning, keep two views of the business. The acquisition view asks =
how new viewers become interested subscribers and customers over time. The =
operating view records the sales and cash actually occurring this month, in=
cluding older relationships. Both matter, but mixing their denominators mak=
es a forecast appear more precise than it is.</p>
<p>If the observed purchase rate is 1% instead of the modeled 5%, the same =
acquisition chain yields twelve orders and $588 gross. That one change redu=
ces notes revenue by $2,352. Before buying more software, ask whether the p=
roduct, audience, evidence, or offer needs improvement. A spreadsheet canno=
t manufacture a 5% rate merely because the revenue target requires it.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-09-section-10"></a></p>
<h3>Demand arithmetic: services have different constraints</h3>
<p>Two $1,500 audits require two qualified clients willing to pay for a def=
ined decision and a provider able to deliver. They do not follow automatica=
lly from the notebook conversion rate. A client may come directly from an e=
ngineering case study, a referral, an existing relationship, or a video. Ea=
ch source has a different level of intent.</p>
<p>For a transparent illustrative chain, suppose a month produces twelve qu=
alified audit inquiries. Eight proceed to a useful scope discussion, and tw=
o become paid engagements. The inquiry-to-engagement rate is 2/12, about 16=
.7%. The discussion-to-engagement rate is 2/8, or 25%. Neither is a benchma=
rk. The model requires inquiries that actually fit the offer; general =E2=
=80=9Ccan you help me code?=E2=80=9D messages are not automatically qualifi=
ed audit leads.</p>
<p>Define a qualified inquiry in advance: a person has a relevant project, =
authority or permission to share the necessary material, a decision the rev=
iew can inform, an appropriate budget, and a timeline you can serve. Avoid =
collecting sensitive documents before fit is established. A video that demo=
nstrates a careful diagnostic process can provide useful proof, but cannot =
replace scope or competence.</p>
<p>Tutoring likewise has a schedule. Ten sessions might come from ten diffe=
rent learners or repeat bookings by fewer people. Report ten sessions as te=
n sessions. If each live hour requires preparation and follow-up, revenue p=
er session is not revenue per total working hour. Record cancellations, no-=
shows, rescheduling, and any refunds separately so you understand the true =
workload.</p>
<p>Do not turn a fixed-scope audit into an unlimited implementation contrac=
t to save a sale. If the client needs a different service, write a new scop=
e and price. You want a business whose promises remain deliverable as deman=
d increases. More inquiries are useful only if you can qualify and serve th=
em without disrupting your core work and content schedule.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-09-section-11"></a></p>
<h3>Recurring revenue is a stock with leakage</h3>
<p>Forty members at $39 produce $1,560 of monthly gross recurring billings =
in the simplified scenario. That does not mean you acquire forty new member=
s every month. It means forty members are paying during the period under th=
e stated billing convention. A real ledger also needs joining dates, cancel=
lations, failed payments, refunds, and annual-plan treatment if you offer a=
nnual billing.</p>
<p>Churn describes departures relative to a defined starting group. If you =
begin with forty members and four leave during the month, a simple member-c=
hurn measure is 4/40, or 10%. If four new people join, you end at forty des=
pite acquiring four members. If ten join while four leave, you end at forty=
-six. Growth requires additions to exceed departures under that convention.=
</p>
<p>This arithmetic explains why a paid group is not passive income. You mus=
t provide continuing value and help suitable new members find the group. A =
member leaving after finishing their project can be a good outcome; the bus=
iness still needs to account for it. Do not keep someone paying through con=
fusing cancellation or guilt. A respected former member may be a valuable r=
eferral source.</p>
<p>A crude lifetime approximation is one divided by a constant monthly chur=
n rate. At 10%, this suggests ten months. Multiplying by $39 gives $390 in =
gross billings per member under restrictive assumptions. This is <strong>no=
t</strong> a reliable early forecast: churn may change by cohort, there may=
 be a trial or annual plan, and gross billings omit fees and delivery costs=
. Use actual cohort observations before spending money to acquire members b=
ased on a projected lifetime value.</p>
<p>The safer starting decision is operational. Can you deliver a defined re=
curring activity in a fixed time box, do people return voluntarily, and do =
they describe a continuing problem it solves? Validate those conditions bef=
ore treating forty paying members as an input you can simply type into the =
target model.</p>
